WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.870
-Our first guest tonight is a
four-time Emmy Award-winning

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.372
actor you know from his work
as Sheldon Cooper

00:00:06.372 --> 00:00:07.773
on "The Big Bang Theory" as well

00:00:07.773 --> 00:00:09.642
as "The Normal Heart"
and "The Boys in the Band."

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:12.312
He stars in "Spoiler Alert,"
which is in select cities

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:15.281
December 2nd and opens
nationwide December 9th.

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:17.317
Let's take a look.

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:19.352
-Okay. You can come out now.

00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:24.089
[ Chuckles ]
Ta-da!

00:00:24.089 --> 00:00:27.126
Our very first Christmas tree
decorated by yours truly.

00:00:27.126 --> 00:00:30.096
-Uh-huh, yes. It's, um --
-What?

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:32.398
-A lot of tinsel.
-There's tinsel on there, yes.

00:00:32.398 --> 00:00:34.934
-Uh-huh, and I see
a Smurf or two.

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:36.536
-There are some Smurfs.
-Yes.

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:39.038
And an entire solar system.

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:40.773
-That's a pretty star.

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:42.708
You hate it.
-I don't hate it.

00:00:42.708 --> 00:00:44.477
-No, you hate it.
-It's very you.

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:45.644
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:45.644 --> 00:00:47.380
our friend Jim Parsons,
everyone.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.348
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:49.348 --> 00:00:56.989 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:56.989 --> 00:01:04.664 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:07.266
Welcome back.
Very happy to have you here.

00:01:07.266 --> 00:01:10.469
-I'm very happy to be here.
-Before we get to this wonderful

00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:12.605
film, Thanksgiving,
you and your husband

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:13.807
are hosting his family.
-His family.

00:01:13.807 --> 00:01:15.008
-Have you done this
over the years?

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:17.644
-How did you know that?
Oh, I told people.

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:20.546
Yes, we've done this.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:01:20.546 --> 00:01:22.181
-I'm not scared.
-Oh, that's wonderful.

00:01:22.181 --> 00:01:23.782
-No, it is.
It's really wonderful.

00:01:23.782 --> 00:01:26.152
-Who is in charge of putting
the meal together?

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:29.388
-Well, it will be mostly
a reheating situation

00:01:29.388 --> 00:01:30.756
since we ordered
from a restaurant.

00:01:30.756 --> 00:01:31.991
-Oh, you ordered
from a restaurant.

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:33.259
-Yes.
-Just one restaurant,

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:34.260
or do you sort of like --
-One.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:35.561
This one's only from one
restaurant.

00:01:35.561 --> 00:01:37.830
-Okay.
-You know, I have shows.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:38.831
I'm working.
-Yes.

00:01:38.831 --> 00:01:40.800
-You know.
-I am very --

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.369
I'm very pro ordering out.
I'm not judging or shaming.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:44.437
I think that's a great plan.

00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:45.939
-It's such a nice way
to spend the day.

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:48.174
It's so much better to get to
sit around and drink with people

00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:49.875
as opposed to slaving
over a turkey.

00:01:49.875 --> 00:01:51.244
-Yes.
-That's my feeling about it.

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:53.546
-And there's just the risk of
getting it wrong.

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:55.114
I feel like a turkey --

00:01:55.114 --> 00:01:56.683
-You may end up ordering
something anyway.

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:59.852
-Yeah, right?
You might as well skip the line.

00:01:59.852 --> 00:02:01.954
But then you are -- it should be
noted you are --

00:02:01.954 --> 00:02:03.722
The tables are turning
for Christmas, right?

00:02:03.722 --> 00:02:06.259
-For Christmas, my family is --
You know a lot about my life.

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:07.460
[ Laughter ]
Yes, my family is

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:08.594
coming for Christmas.
-My apartment's right

00:02:08.594 --> 00:02:10.596
across from yours,
so I'm watching.

00:02:10.596 --> 00:02:12.798
-Binoculars at work, yes.

00:02:12.798 --> 00:02:14.433
Yes, they'll be out
for Christmas.

00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:17.903
-You and your husband, Todd,
have been together 20 years.

00:02:17.903 --> 00:02:19.339
-Yes.
-I know I keep dropping details.

00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:20.606
-No, I don't mind. I don't mind.

00:02:20.606 --> 00:02:21.707
[ Cheers and applause ]
Thank you.

00:02:21.707 --> 00:02:23.676
-You had an anniversary.

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:26.712
That's a big anniversary.
-Yes, it was huge.

00:02:26.712 --> 00:02:29.882
May I say, kind of related to
that -- I was going to say this

00:02:29.882 --> 00:02:34.320
earlier is that Jenny's speech,
so beautifully put.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.857
And I feel like for anyone who
doesn't understand the impact

00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:39.592
that some of --
I don't know what to call it --

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:43.162
negativity or bias that gets
pumped out there can cause,

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:46.966
a 49-year-old man in
a 20-year relationship, out,

00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:50.470
proud with a successful career,
is still moved to near tears

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:51.771
at hearing someone
like that say,

00:02:51.771 --> 00:02:53.972
"I see you and you're great
just as you are."

00:02:53.972 --> 00:02:57.209
I -- I don't -- I don't think
you can ever oversell

00:02:57.209 --> 00:03:00.346
how impactful it is,
those messages of negativity,

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:02.314
and that doesn't --
it doesn't --

00:03:02.314 --> 00:03:04.283
it doesn't really go away.
You just learn how to --

00:03:04.283 --> 00:03:06.218
where to put it and how to
own it for yourself.

00:03:06.218 --> 00:03:07.620
So thank you, Jenny.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.088
-Well, we're very lucky
to have her.

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:12.057
-Yes.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:12.057 --> 00:03:14.894
Sorry to sidetrack.
-No, no.

00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:16.395
-I didn't know that was
going to happen.

00:03:16.395 --> 00:03:20.432
It was just very well done.
-Thank you for saying that.

00:03:20.432 --> 00:03:22.902
I had nothing to do with it.
-I believe that.

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:25.938
-You -- How did you guys meet
20 years ago?

00:03:25.938 --> 00:03:27.573
-Blind date.
-Oh, wow!

00:03:27.573 --> 00:03:31.110
-His boss and a woman I went to
grad school with were very close

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:35.013
friends, and they just set it
up, and they went with us.

00:03:35.013 --> 00:03:38.150
And I remember we went to
a pool hall, and -- which I --

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:40.152
I enjoy playing pool.
He hates it.

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:42.121
So we went from there
to karaoke,

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:44.256
which he enjoys and I hate.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.256 --> 00:03:46.091
And then finally he was like,
"I have to go.

00:03:46.091 --> 00:03:47.826
I have a friend who's having
a birthday party

00:03:47.826 --> 00:03:49.395
at a gay bar across town.

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:51.930
You want to come?"
And so we finally ditched them,

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.266
and we went to this gay bar for
this birthday party together,

00:03:54.266 --> 00:03:55.802
and that was where it was like,

00:03:55.802 --> 00:03:57.269
"Oh, we're going to do this
again."

00:03:57.269 --> 00:03:59.805
And we did. And you know what
our first date alone was?

00:03:59.805 --> 00:04:01.407
Was watching
"Bowling for Columbine."

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:02.542
-Really?

00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:05.077
-Yes, which I never found
strange.

00:04:05.077 --> 00:04:06.412
He still finds it -- He's like,

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:09.148
"I cannot believe
that was the suggestion."

00:04:09.148 --> 00:04:11.617
But, I mean, you know --
-You got to see movies.

00:04:11.617 --> 00:04:14.487
-You got to go to a movie.
-So do you --

00:04:14.487 --> 00:04:17.657
Has karaoke and pool halls
become a part of

00:04:17.657 --> 00:04:19.092
your relationship over
the last 20 years, or --

00:04:19.092 --> 00:04:20.626
-Not in a good way, no.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.626 --> 00:04:22.762
I mean, no, we hardly ever go.

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:25.030
I've gone to karaoke
a couple times and stomached it,

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.032
but I just, you know.
It's not my thing.

00:04:27.032 --> 00:04:29.902
-I am very much one of you.
-You're not loving karaoke.

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:31.870
-I am not,
and my wife loves karaoke.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:34.607
-Well, we -- Look,
in their defense, we have --

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:36.509
I don't know what
your wife does necessarily --

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:38.678
but we have a chance to perform.
-That is true.

00:04:38.678 --> 00:04:40.179
-We don't need the time
in the room

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:42.248
with the microphone in that way.

00:04:42.248 --> 00:04:44.250
-That is a very nice
observation.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:45.818
-I get that out of my system
otherwise.

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:48.488
-Yeah. Now, I should note
back in my "SNL" days,

00:04:48.488 --> 00:04:50.857
sometimes the after-after-party
would be at a karaoke bar.

00:04:50.857 --> 00:04:52.190
-Oh.
-And that would be a time

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:54.694
where my fellow castmates,
who I had to [bleep] watch

00:04:54.694 --> 00:04:56.762
all week onstage --
-They would sing then?

00:04:56.762 --> 00:04:58.197
-They would do it again,
and I'd be like --

00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:00.433
-The majority of the people
there enjoyed doing karaoke?

00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:02.535
-Yeah.
-So telling. So telling.

00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:03.902
[ Laughter ]

00:05:03.902 --> 00:05:05.671
-Criminal!
One would say maybe criminal.

00:05:05.671 --> 00:05:07.906
-No wonder you left.
-I had to, yeah.

00:05:07.906 --> 00:05:10.009
That's why I left.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:12.478
After 12 1/2 years, I'm like,
"I've had it!"

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:14.780
[ Laughter ]

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:17.150
Do you have a
20th anniversary --

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:19.852
Do you do something grand?
-No, we didn't.

00:05:19.852 --> 00:05:21.387
I'll tell you -- Well, sort of.

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:24.122
Like, I mean, we were
in San Francisco

00:05:24.122 --> 00:05:27.093
for a special screening of
"Spoiler Alert" on Monday,

00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:28.260
and Tuesday was the anniversary.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:29.895
So we were on an airplane,

00:05:29.895 --> 00:05:31.831
and then I came home and went
immediately to the show,

00:05:31.831 --> 00:05:34.133
and then we went to bed,
so that was how we "celebrated."

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:38.170
But I will say since Todd was --
My husband was the first one who

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:40.806
said we should adapt "Spoiler
Alert" and get the rights

00:05:40.806 --> 00:05:42.241
and we did
and we saw it through,

00:05:42.241 --> 00:05:44.711
it was kind of like
a really nice present.

00:05:44.711 --> 00:05:48.447
-That is really nice.
This is a very --

00:05:48.447 --> 00:05:51.284
This is based on a wonderful
book by Michael Ausiello,

00:05:51.284 --> 00:05:53.218
who is an entertainment reporter

00:05:53.218 --> 00:05:55.354
who we've both been lucky enough
to meet over the years.

00:05:55.354 --> 00:05:57.657
He wrote this very moving book

00:05:57.657 --> 00:05:59.959
about his partner
who passed away.

00:05:59.959 --> 00:06:02.227
-Yeah.
-And his relationship with him.

00:06:02.227 --> 00:06:05.264
And this is a -- How did you
first come to read the book?

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:09.035
-Michael asked me to host a Q&amp;A
at a Barnes &amp; Noble for him,

00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:11.971
and he sent me the book,
and I sat there for --

00:06:11.971 --> 00:06:14.306
I said, "Of course I will,"
but I hadn't read it yet.

00:06:14.306 --> 00:06:17.676
We were taking a vacation, Todd
and I, and I read it, and Todd

00:06:17.676 --> 00:06:20.346
watched me read it, and I just
was like -- [ Cries ]

00:06:20.346 --> 00:06:22.148
And he said, "Do you think it
would be a good movie?"

00:06:22.148 --> 00:06:25.651
I went, "I don't know."
So he read it, also on a plane

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:28.120
somewhere where the stewardesses
were like, "Are you okay?"

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:31.724
And he said, "We should option
this if he'll let us."

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:33.492
And so at the Q&amp;A, he went up to
Michael and said,

00:06:33.492 --> 00:06:35.895
"Can we option it?"
And ball rolling.

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:38.197
-Obviously Michael held you
in some regard

00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:39.564
to ask you to do the Q&amp;A.
-Yeah.

00:06:39.564 --> 00:06:41.534
-But you still now are --

00:06:41.534 --> 00:06:43.736
You know, you're sort of
the steward of his story.

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:45.204
Do you feel an added pressure

00:06:45.204 --> 00:06:47.973
when you play a real-life person
on-screen?

00:06:47.973 --> 00:06:49.708
-A little bit.

00:06:49.708 --> 00:06:53.278
Mostly I take it as a real gift
because there's so much rich

00:06:53.278 --> 00:06:55.848
information that you wouldn't
get from a fictional character.

00:06:55.848 --> 00:06:58.918
And especially with a book like
Michael had written, I mean,

00:06:58.918 --> 00:07:01.053
he poured so much of himself
into it and he was just --

00:07:01.053 --> 00:07:03.122
It was very clear.

00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:06.792
The part of the pressure would
have been worse, but Michael was

00:07:06.792 --> 00:07:09.395
so sane about --
He said a thousand times,

00:07:09.395 --> 00:07:12.331
"I understand that what's being
portrayed in the movie

00:07:12.331 --> 00:07:15.635
isn't identical to my life,
and I don't expect it to be."

00:07:15.635 --> 00:07:17.469
And I think everyone needed to
hear that, though,

00:07:17.469 --> 00:07:18.704
because it's such a deep tale.

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:21.974
It's such a -- There's so much
heart and tragedy

00:07:21.974 --> 00:07:24.710
and joy in it that, um --
Yeah, you don't want to --

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.345
You don't want to get it wrong.

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:29.415
-You let the audience know
in advance

00:07:29.415 --> 00:07:31.216
what kind of movie
they were seeing.

00:07:31.216 --> 00:07:34.119
You were handing out
sort of branded tissues.

00:07:34.119 --> 00:07:37.255
-I didn't personally
hand them out.

00:07:37.255 --> 00:07:39.158
But, yes, I did see them.

00:07:39.158 --> 00:07:40.559
There's these little
packs of tissues.

00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:41.627
Yeah, there's some tears.

00:07:41.627 --> 00:07:43.262
I said I cried when I read it.

00:07:43.262 --> 00:07:45.932
If we didn't make anybody cry,
we wouldn't have done our jobs.

00:07:45.932 --> 00:07:49.368
-Yeah. You're also back onstage.

00:07:49.368 --> 00:07:51.470
-Yes.
-Doing a revival,

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:52.839
"A Man of No Importance."
-Yes.

00:07:52.839 --> 00:07:55.040
-You play a Dublin bus driver.
-Yes.

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:56.242
-That means accent work.

00:07:56.242 --> 00:07:58.110
-[ In Irish accent ]
Indeed it does.

00:07:58.110 --> 00:07:59.912
-Oh! Really good.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:59.912 --> 00:08:02.481
-I swore I wouldn't do it on TV,
but here I am.

00:08:02.481 --> 00:08:05.551
Givin' ya me best Dublin accent
I can.

00:08:05.551 --> 00:08:09.622
-That's very good.
-Thank you, but no, Seth. No.

00:08:09.622 --> 00:08:11.757
[ Laughter ]
You're very polite.

00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:13.826
-Oh, my God.
Jim, bring Jim back!

00:08:13.826 --> 00:08:15.261
-It's a completely different
pairson.

00:08:15.261 --> 00:08:16.962
[ Normal voice ] Alright.
I'm back. I'm back.

00:08:16.962 --> 00:08:18.964
Oh, God.
-A completely different pairson.

00:08:18.964 --> 00:08:21.567
[ Laughter ]
-Parsons. Pairsons.

00:08:21.567 --> 00:08:23.669
-Oh, that must be tricky with
your last name being Parsons.

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:26.005
-Yes, awful.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:08:26.005 --> 00:08:27.874
This is very lovely as well.

00:08:27.874 --> 00:08:31.110
And very sweet of you to do --
everyone in the audience tonight

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:34.112
will be getting vouchers to go
see "Spoiler Alert."

00:08:34.112 --> 00:08:35.914
-Yes.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:35.914 --> 00:08:38.750
-We really appreciate it.

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:42.954
Oh, there's one -- Again,
very moving story, real life,

00:08:42.954 --> 00:08:45.657
not my place to be critical of
any part of it.

00:08:45.657 --> 00:08:47.926
I do have a very small cameo
in the film.

00:08:47.926 --> 00:08:50.196
-Yes, you do!

00:08:50.196 --> 00:08:52.298
And what's funny is I never knew
it was going to be there

00:08:52.298 --> 00:08:54.166
because I was acting to
a blank TV screen.

00:08:54.166 --> 00:08:55.801
And when I first saw the cut,
I was like,

00:08:55.801 --> 00:08:58.104
"Ah, that's brilliant."
You're talking about somebody

00:08:58.104 --> 00:08:59.839
smuggling Xanax into a doughnut.

00:08:59.839 --> 00:09:02.407
-Yes, but you don't stay till --

00:09:02.407 --> 00:09:03.809
You show the beginning
of the joke.

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:05.811
-I turn it off.
-You turn off the TV.

00:09:05.811 --> 00:09:07.146
-I don't want to distract
from the story

00:09:07.146 --> 00:09:08.847
with your good punch--
or whatever punchline.

00:09:08.847 --> 00:09:10.315
Maybe it was good.
Maybe it was mediocre.

00:09:10.315 --> 00:09:11.550
-That's very kind of you to say

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:13.118
that it was going to be
a good punchline.

00:09:13.118 --> 00:09:16.555
But it makes me feel better
knowing that you, a dear friend

00:09:16.555 --> 00:09:19.257
of this show, didn't know when
you were filming it that

00:09:19.257 --> 00:09:21.259
you were going to turn the TV
off on me.

00:09:21.259 --> 00:09:22.928
-Yeah. No, of course not.

00:09:22.928 --> 00:09:24.463
I would have picketed.

00:09:24.463 --> 00:09:26.699
[ Laughter ]

00:09:26.699 --> 00:09:28.533
-Hey, it's always such
a pleasure to see you.

00:09:28.533 --> 00:09:30.268
-It's great to be here.
-Have the happiest of holidays.

00:09:30.268 --> 00:09:32.137
-You too. Thank you.
-And congrats on the film.

00:09:32.137 --> 00:09:33.972
You guys, that's Jim Parsons.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:33.972 --> 00:09:36.776
"Spoiler Alert" opens
in select cities

00:09:36.776 --> 00:09:38.877
December 2nd and nationwide
December 9th.

00:09:38.877 --> 00:09:41.047
We'll be right back with
Amber Ruffin and Lacey Lamar.

